# Development

[AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md) holds the rules nothing checks. This is the root of the tree, the gate
and the tiers, the comment ban, how to work in one half, and how a script stops what it
started. [frontend.md](frontend.md) and [backend.md](backend.md) hold the two halves.

## The two halves

Each half installs, lints, tests and builds without the other's toolchain: `pnpm` runs in
`frontend/`, `uv` in `backend/`, and `make` at the root fans out to both. There is no root
`package.json`, and `pnpm install` at the root is meant to fail.

```
openapi.json          the contract -- generated from the backend, committed
Makefile              the interface across both halves
CONTEXT.md            the vocabulary
docs/adr/             architectural decisions, as its README defines them;
                      template/ holds the ones that came with the template
frontend/             everything pnpm touches
backend/              everything uv touches
deploy/               schema.sql and roles.sql -- generated from app/store/
```

- **The `Makefile` exports what `docker compose` reads.** Compose reads `DB_PORT`,
  `APP_DB_PASSWORD` and the others from its environment, and a make variable is not in it. Do not
  put `VAR=value` in front of the recipe line instead: a password that holds a quote or a space
  breaks the command. The `DATABASE_URL` that `make db` prints does not percent-encode the
  password.
- **The `Makefile` sets `UV_EXCLUDE_NEWER` as well as `backend/pyproject.toml`.** `uvx` runs
  outside the project and does not read the pyproject.
- **`make install` fetches what the gate and the browser tiers need.** It fetches gitleaks, the
  pinned openapi-typescript and Chromium last, after it arms the hooks. The gate needs the first
  two. Without them, the first commit needs the network, and fails without one on a check that
  is not about the network. Both browser tiers need Chromium. Neither tier fetches it, so
  `make test-e2e` runs in a sandbox with no network. The cost is a browser download on the first
  `make install` and after each change to the Playwright version, even for a developer who never
  runs a browser tier. Run `make install` again after `make upgrade`, or the browser tiers find
  no browser. `E2E_BROWSERS` in the `Makefile` names the browsers it fetches. A project that runs
  the browser tiers on more than Chromium widens it there, and names each in the `projects` of
  `frontend/playwright.config.ts`.

## The gate

{% if github_ci -%}
- **The gate is `make gate`:** secrets, lint-check, openapi-check and test. The git hook runs
  it through `make pre-commit` before a commit, and `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs it directly
  after a push, against a fresh checkout nobody configured. A new check goes in `make gate`,
  never into the workflow: a workflow that re-lists the steps is a second copy, and the copy is
  the one that goes stale.
{%- else -%}
- **The gate is `make gate`:** secrets, lint-check, openapi-check and test. The git hook runs
  it through `make pre-commit` before a commit. If your CI runs it after a push, have it run
  `make gate` rather than a list of the same steps: a copy of the list is the one that goes
  stale.
{%- endif %}
- **`make pre-commit` runs the gate once per tree.** `devtools/gate.sh` queues behind any other
  gate of this project and records the tree a green run read, so a commit of that same tree
  passes at once. Edit one byte and the gate runs again.
  [adr/template/0014](adr/template/0014-the-gate-runs-once-per-tree-and-one-at-a-time.md) says
  why.
- **`devtools/gate.sh` unsets git's local environment first.** git runs the hook with `GIT_DIR`
  and `GIT_INDEX_FILE` set to this repository. A test that runs `git init` in a temporary
  directory then reinitialises this repository instead. Keep the `unset`.
- **`make hooks` writes a shim, and does not set `core.hooksPath`.** It writes one shim per file
  in `.githooks/` into git's hooks directory, so the hook body stays under version control. Every
  worktree shares `core.hooksPath`, and the key turns off the hooks that other tools installed.
  `make hooks` moves a hook that was there first to `<name>.local`, then `<name>.local.2`, and
  the shim runs each one before this project's hook. It finds every slot already taken before it
  moves one, so a new hook never replaces a chained one. It refuses a `core.hooksPath` that
  points anywhere else, and compares the two paths after it resolves them: macOS reaches `/var`
  as `/private/var`.
- **`make secrets` is in the gate.** gitleaks reads the tree the gate certifies. Remove a finding
  and rotate the secret. When it is no secret at all, put its fingerprint in `.gitleaksignore`.
- **No test skips itself.** A test that needs a daemon or a browser goes in a tier declared in
  `backend/tests/tiers.py`. Everything else mirrors the source it covers. A skip exits 0 and
  looks like a pass, so `test_gate.py` fails on one.
- **A complexity threshold is a reviewable decision.** Raising one, or recording a baseline
  upward, is not the way to green. `make lint` lowers a stale baseline for you, and records again
  one that the codebase moved by changing size rather than shape; either change belongs in your
  commit.

## The tiers

`make test-e2e`, `make test-e2e-live`, `make db-test` and `make observe-test` are deliberately
outside the gate. Each needs something fetched or started first, and people skip a gate that
needs a browser or a daemon.

{% if github_ci -%}
`ci.yml` runs `db-test` in a job of its own after a push. Nothing runs the others, so nothing
tells you that you did not run them: `test-e2e` after a UI change, `observe-test` after touching
`app/telemetry.py`. Run `db-test` yourself after touching `app/store/` or `deploy/` too, because
a failure after a push reaches you late.
{%- else -%}
Nothing runs them, so nothing tells you that you did not run them: `db-test` after touching
`app/store/` or `deploy/`, `test-e2e` after a UI change, `observe-test` after touching
`app/telemetry.py`. `db-test` needs only Docker or the Postgres server binaries, so it is the
tier to add to your CI first.
{%- endif %}

- **`make test-contract-db` runs the contract suite on Postgres, as `app_app`.** A superuser
  bypasses row-level security, so a run on the superuser's connection passes against a database
  with no policy in force. Keep the application role.
- **`make db-test` uses `TEST_DATABASE_URL` when it is set**, and starts no server.
- **Without a running Docker, `make db-test` starts a server from `initdb` and `pg_ctl`.** It
  finds them on `PATH`, or in the folder that `pg_config --bindir` names, because Debian keeps
  them off `PATH`. The server runs the Postgres version you installed, not the one in
  `DB_TEST_IMAGE`, and the first line `make db-test` prints names that version. It listens only
  on a Unix socket in a folder under `/tmp`, not under `$TMPDIR`, because `$TMPDIR` can be long
  and a socket path longer than about 100 bytes fails.
- **Each tier's container takes an ephemeral port and a name that holds the shell's PID.** With a
  fixed port or name, two checkouts that run a tier at once fail, or one deletes the other's
  database.
- **The `Makefile` runs its recipes in bash.** dash runs an `EXIT INT TERM` trap only after the
  foreground command returns. On Ctrl-C it never returns, and the container leaks.

## The comment ban

`make lint` fails on any comment token under `frontend/{src,tests,e2e,devtools}`, and on any
comment in a Python file, shell script, Perl script, `Makefile` or `.githooks/` hook that git
sees anywhere in the project. `frontend/devtools/comments.mjs` and `backend/devtools/comments.py`
are the two gates. A script's heredoc body is data, and the gate does not read it. A script that
ends inside a quote or a heredoc is refused, because the gate cannot tell where its comments are.

- **Suppressions are refused too:** `biome-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, `@ts-ignore`, `# noqa`,
  `# type: ignore`. A suppression is a threshold decision taken silently, where the check fails.
  Make it a fix in the code, or a reviewable line in `biome.json`, `tsconfig.json` or
  `pyproject.toml`. ruff's `BLE` refuses `except Exception`, and the one place a blind catch is
  right carries a line under `per-file-ignores`.
- **Shebangs and TypeScript `///` directives are not comments.** They are executable
  directives, and neither gate reads them.
- **A Python docstring is not a comment.** It is a string bound to the symbol, reachable through
  `help()`. A module, class or function may carry one. `/** */` is a comment token, JSDoc
  included.
- **Config files may carry comments** where the format offers no other way to explain a rule.
  That includes `frontend/*.config.ts`. Vendored and generated code is out of scope entirely.

## Working in one half

The commands in `AGENTS.md` cover the common cases, and the `Makefile` is the full list: the
tiers, the migration targets and the per-half variants. `make observe` starts Grafana, Tempo and
Prometheus and prints the `OTEL_*` variables to export.

`BACKEND_PORT`, `FRONTEND_PORT` and `PREVIEW_PORT` move every server, so a second checkout can run
at the same time. `make test-contract` takes two free ports unless you set them, so the gate does
not fail while another checkout serves on 8000 or 5173.

Each half also works with its own tools: `pnpm -C frontend test:watch`,
`pnpm -C frontend add some-package`, `cd backend && uv run pytest -q tests/routes/`,
`cd backend && uv add some-package`.

## Stopping what a script started

`devtools/dev.sh` and `devtools/contract-test.sh` start servers and must stop them. Each rule
below fails only later, as a server that holds its port.

- **Stop a process group, not a pid.** `uv run` and `pnpm` exec a launcher that starts the server
  as a child. A kill of the launcher's pid leaves the server running. `own_group` makes the group
  with `os.setsid()`, because dash turns `set -m` off when no terminal is attached. `setsid`
  refuses with `EPERM` in a process that already leads its group. The assertion after the
  `except` is what keeps that refusal from hiding an ungrouped process.
- **Write `kill -TERM "-$pid"`, never `kill -TERM -- "-$pid"`.** dash's `kill` refuses `--` and
  sends nothing. bash, which is `/bin/sh` on macOS, accepts it, so the leak shows only on Linux.
  Never add a single-pid kill after the group kill: it hides both failures.
- **Send `TERM`, wait a second, then send `KILL`.** uvicorn's `--reload` supervisor does not
  always exit after its worker, and `uv run` waits for it.
- **Give every health probe `--max-time`.** A server that listens and never replies holds an
  unbounded `curl` past the deadline. An app that fails to import does this under `--reload`.
- **`dev.sh` runs vite in the foreground, without `exec`.** `exec` discards the cleanup trap, and
  the backend keeps its port after vite exits. `set -m` gives vite the terminal, so its keyboard
  shortcuts work.
- **`contract-test.sh` takes both ports in one process.** Two separate requests can return the
  same port, and the gate then fails at random.
